    conds:[{n:'filtration',v:[78,100,1413],r:'<b>Filtration</b>: insoluble solid + liquid (sand + salt water) · fold filter paper into a funnel, pour the mixture through · <b>residue</b> (sand) stays on the paper, <b>filtrate</b> (solution) passes through · first step in making salts (remove excess base)'},{n:'crystallisation',v:[78,100,1413],r:'<b>Crystallisation</b>: heat the solution in an evaporating basin to evaporate some water until crystals start to form (crystallisation point), then leave to cool; filter and dry the crystals · gives well-formed crystals and doesn’t decompose heat-sensitive salts (unlike evaporating to dryness)'},{n:'simple distillation',v:[78,100,1413],r:'<b>Simple distillation</b>: separate a <b>solvent</b> from a solution — salt water: water (bp 100 °C) boils, vapour condenses in the Liebig condenser (cold water in at the bottom) and is collected; salt (bp 1413 °C) stays behind · thermometer bulb at the side arm reads the boiling point of what distils'},{n:'fractional distillation',v:[78,100,1413],r:'<b>Fractional distillation</b>: mixture of liquids with close boiling points, e.g. ethanol (78 °C) and water (100 °C) · the <b>fractionating column</b> (glass beads) has a temperature gradient — vapour repeatedly condenses and re-evaporates; ethanol reaches the top first · collect fractions at different temperatures · used for crude oil and liquid air'}],
