    conds:[{n:'aim and hypothesis',v:[0,0,0,0,0],r:'<b>Aim</b>: how does the concentration of sodium thiosulfate affect the rate of its reaction with hydrochloric acid? · <b>hypothesis</b>: increasing concentration increases the rate, because particles are closer together so there are more frequent successful collisions · the prediction is testable and measurable'},{n:'variables, range and repeats',v:[160,80,53,40,32],r:'<b>Independent</b>: [Na₂S₂O₃] — five values 0.05–0.25 mol dm⁻³ (a sensible range and interval) · <b>dependent</b>: time for the cross to disappear (bars, s) · <b>controls</b>: volume and concentration of HCl, total volume, temperature, same cross and observer · <b>repeat</b> each three times to spot anomalies and calculate a mean'},{n:'choosing apparatus',v:[160,80,53,40,32],r:'<b>Resolution</b> matters: burette (±0.05 cm³) or pipette rather than a measuring cylinder (±0.5 cm³) for small volumes · stopwatch (±0.01 s, but reaction time ~0.2 s) or a light sensor/data logger · thermometer or water bath to control temperature · a gas syringe for gas volumes · balance to 2–3 decimal places'},{n:'risk assessment',v:[160,80,53,40,32],r:'<b>Hazard → risk → control</b>: HCl is an irritant/corrosive — goggles, wipe spills · SO₂ produced is toxic, may trigger asthma — good ventilation, small volumes, dispose of in a fume cupboard · glassware breakage — sweep with a brush · <b>disposal</b>: neutralise acids, don’t pour heavy-metal solutions down the sink'}],
