    conds:[{n:'addition polymers',v:[40,30,30],r:'<b>Addition polymerisation</b>: n CH₂=CHCH₃ → –(CH₂–CH(CH₃))ₙ– poly(propene) · C=C breaks, no other product · repeat unit: two main-chain carbons in brackets with n · <b>monomer from repeat unit</b>: put a C=C between the two backbone carbons · PVC from chloroethene, PTFE from tetrafluoroethene'},{n:'condensation polymers',v:[40,30,30],r:'<b>Polyesters</b>: diol + dicarboxylic acid (or diacyl chloride) → –COO– links + H₂O (or HCl) — e.g. Terylene/PET · <b>polyamides</b>: diamine + dicarboxylic acid → –CONH– links — nylon-6,6, Kevlar · <b>monomers from repeat unit</b>: split each link, add –OH to the C=O side and –H to the O or N side'},{n:'biodegradability',v:[40,30,30],r:'<b>Poly(alkenes)</b>: non-polar saturated chains, chemically inert → not biodegradable, persist for centuries · <b>polyesters/polyamides</b>: polar C=O in ester/amide links attacked by nucleophiles → <b>hydrolysed</b> by acid (gives the acid + diol/ammonium salt) or alkali (carboxylate salt + diol/diamine) · some (PLA from lactic acid) are biodegradable'},{n:'disposal',v:[20,30,50],r:'<b>Landfill</b>: takes space, persists · <b>incineration</b>: releases energy for electricity, but makes CO₂ and toxic gases (HCl from PVC — removed with bases in scrubbers) · <b>recycling</b>: sorting, melting and remoulding (saves crude oil, energy) · <b>feedstock recycling</b>: cracking polymers back to monomers/chemicals · chemists develop biodegradable and photodegradable polymers'}],
