    conds:[{n:'ageing population',v:[18,63,19],r:'<b>Ageing population</b> (UK 65+ ≈ 19%, rising to ~25% by 2040): demand grows for healthcare, care homes, leisure, travel, mobility aids, financial planning · the “grey pound” is a big, wealthy segment · labour shortages → firms recruit older workers, automate, offer flexible hours · pension costs'},{n:'future structure',v:[16,59,25],r:'<b>Projected 2040</b>: fewer young people, smaller working-age share → products for children and students face slower growth; labour costs may rise · firms adapt products (larger text, easy-to-use tech), marketing images and store design'},{n:'lifestyle and attitudes',v:[18,63,19],r:'<b>Social trends</b>: health and fitness (low-sugar, plant-based, gyms), convenience (delivery apps, ready meals), ethical and sustainable buying, remote and flexible working, diversity and inclusion expectations · opportunities for new products, threats to firms that ignore them (sugar tax + changing tastes for soft drinks)'},{n:'social media and migration',v:[18,63,19],r:'<b>Social media</b>: reviews and influencers shape demand quickly; viral trends (and backlashes) can make or break products · <b>migration</b>: increases labour supply and skills, creates demand for new foods and services · <b>household changes</b>: more single-person households → smaller pack sizes, smaller homes'}],
