    conds:[{n:'class boxes',v:[4,6,7],r:'<b>Class diagram box</b>: name / attributes / methods · visibility: <b>+</b> public, <b>−</b> private, <b>#</b> protected · attributes with types (− age: int), methods with parameters and return types (+ getName(): String) · encapsulation: private attributes accessed via public getters/setters'},{n:'inheritance',v:[4,6,7],r:'<b>Inheritance</b> (“is a”): Dog and Cat inherit name, age and move() from Animal (arrow with a hollow triangle pointing to the superclass) · subclasses add attributes/methods (bark()) and <b>override</b> methods (Dog.move()) → <b>polymorphism</b>: calling move() on any Animal runs the right version'},{n:'association, aggregation, composition',v:[4,6,7],r:'<b>Association</b>: a link between classes (Owner — Animal) with multiplicity (1 owner, 0..* pets) · <b>aggregation</b> (hollow diamond): “has a”, parts can exist independently (Team has Players) · <b>composition</b> (filled diamond): strong ownership, parts destroyed with the whole (House has Rooms)'},{n:'from design to code',v:[4,6,7],r:'<b>Code</b>: class Dog(Animal): def __init__(self, name, age, breed): super().__init__(name, age); self.__breed = breed · design first → clearer responsibilities, reuse through inheritance and composition, easier team work · principle: favour composition over deep inheritance trees'}],
