    conds:[{n:'the three Vs',v:[2,15,64,180],r:'<b>Big data</b>: too large/complex for one machine or a traditional relational database · <b>volume</b> (petabytes), <b>velocity</b> (streams in real time — sensors, social media, transactions), <b>variety</b> (structured tables + unstructured text, images, video) · bars: global data created per year (zettabytes, approx.)'},{n:'distributed processing',v:[2,15,64,180],r:'<b>Distributed</b>: data split across many servers (clusters); processing happens where the data is · relational databases struggle (fixed schema, single server) · NoSQL stores, fact-based models (immutable facts with timestamps) and graph schemas (nodes, edges, properties) are used instead'},{n:'why functional programming?',v:[2,15,64,180],r:'<b>Functional programming</b>: pure functions (no side effects), immutable data, statelessness → computations can run <b>in parallel</b> on many cores/machines without conflicts or locking · results are deterministic and easier to test · higher-order functions express data transformations concisely'},{n:'map, filter, reduce',v:[2,15,64,180],r:'<b>MapReduce</b>: <b>map</b> applies a function to each chunk on different nodes (e.g. count words in each document) → <b>reduce</b> (fold) combines partial results (add the counts) · filter keeps items meeting a condition · e.g. reduce(add, map(len, filter(isValid, records))) · used by Hadoop, Spark'}],
